---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

// Practical help for fasting, for the fasting book to point at. A
// newcomer's first Great Lent is the hard one: they know what is not
// allowed and nobody has told them what to actually eat. This page is
// the answer to that, and is built to grow — each section stands alone,
// so another can be added without rearranging anything.

// Grouped the way a person shops rather than by protein content: the
// numbers are per different serving sizes (a cup, an ounce, a block)
// and ranking them against each other would compare unlike things.
// Figures are Theia's, from the USDA, kept exactly as given.
const proteinGroups = [
  {
    group: "Beans & Lentils",
    note: "The backbone of fasting cooking in every Orthodox country.",
    foods: [
      { food: "Lentils", protein: "18 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Chickpeas", protein: "20 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Pinto beans", protein: "15 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Black beans", protein: "14 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Peas", protein: "8 g", per: "per cup" },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Soy",
    note: "The most protein for the money, and it takes on any flavor you give it.",
    foods: [
      { food: "Tofu", protein: "36 g", per: "per block" },
      { food: "Tempeh", protein: "31 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Edamame", protein: "17 g", per: "per cup" },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Nuts & Seeds",
    note: "Easy to keep in the house, and the answer to the mid-afternoon slump.",
    foods: [
      { food: "Pumpkin seeds", protein: "12 g", per: "per cup" },
      { food: "Hemp hearts", protein: "9.5 g", per: "per 3 tablespoons" },
      { food: "Almonds", protein: "7 g", per: "per ¼ cup" },
      { food: "Peanuts", protein: "7 g", per: "per ounce" },
      { food: "Pistachios", protein: "6 g", per: "per ounce" },
      { food: "Chia seeds", protein: "4.5 g", per: "per ounce" },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "Grains",
    note: "A base that carries protein of its own, unlike white rice.",
    foods: [{ food: "Quinoa", protein: "8 g", per: "per cup" }],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Keeping the Fast | An Orthodox Table"
  description="Practical help for Orthodox fasting — plant-based protein, and what to actually eat when meat and dairy are set aside."
>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 md:px-6">
    <p class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">
      <a href="/table/" class="text-brand-tan-dark no-underline hover:underline">← An Orthodox Table</a>
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl text-text-primary md:text-4xl">Keeping the Fast</h1>
    <div class="mt-5 h-px w-24 bg-liturgical-current"></div>

    <!-- Don't rank anybody's fasts for them. An earlier opening here
         named one of them as the difficult one; the Nativity Fast is
         longer and is about to begin, and a person keeping it knows
         perfectly well that it is hard. What helps is saying what to
         eat. (The history is in git if the old wording is ever
         wanted.) -->
    <p class="mt-6 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
      Knowing what to eat during a fast is often harder than keeping it. What
      is set aside, any product that came from an animal with a bone, is
      general knowledge. What to put in its place is usually more difficult to
      navigate. Without that information, the fast turns into a list of things
      you cannot have and becomes increasingly difficult to keep.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
      That is what this page is for. Most of the difficulty in fasting is not
      in the cooking — it is our own minds, and our thinking of the fast as
      something being taken away from us. It is simply a different way of
      eating rather than a punishment, and there have never been better vegan
      options than there are now. A fasting meal can be as good as any other
      meal, and when it is, what you are going without stops being the thing
      you notice, and your focus can be on your willing sacrifice to God, as is
      the goal.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
      Below we will go over: where the protein comes from, and who to speak to
      if you cannot keep the fast. The cooking itself — what to keep in, how to shop, and the one
      or two techniques worth learning early — is in
      <a href="/table/vegan-eating/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">An Introduction to Vegan Eating</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- The chief worry of anyone new to it, answered with numbers. -->
    <section class="mt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary">Where the protein comes from</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 h-px w-16 bg-liturgical-current"></div>
      <p class="mt-4 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        It's the first thing almost everyone asks, and it's easier than you'd
        think — a cup of lentils has more protein than three eggs. Keep two or
        three of these in the house and it stops being something to worry
        about.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 space-y-8">
        {
          proteinGroups.map((group) => (
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-display text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-tan-dark">
                {group.group}
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm italic text-text-secondary">{group.note}</p>
              <table class="mt-3 w-full border-collapse text-sm">
                <tbody>
                  {group.foods.map((row) => (
                    <tr class="border-b border-border-soft">
                      <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-text-primary">{row.food}</td>
                      <td class="w-20 py-2 text-right font-semibold text-text-primary">
                        {row.protein}
                      </td>
                      <td class="w-40 py-2 pl-4 text-text-secondary">{row.per}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="mt-5 text-xs text-text-secondary">
        Figures from the USDA. The serving sizes aren't the same — a block of
        tofu isn't a cup of lentils — so read each row on its own instead of
        comparing them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- The cooking itself lives on its own page now: it grew past
         the size of an aside and is a different kind of help from
         this page, which answers what the fast asks of you rather
         than how to cook under it. -->
    <section class="mt-12 border border-border-soft bg-cream-card p-5">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl text-text-primary">New to cooking this way?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-text-primary/85 leading-relaxed">
        Knowing what the fast asks is one thing; knowing what to put on the
        table is another. The introduction covers the food itself &mdash;
        starting with tofu, which almost everybody has had badly and which is
        the nearest thing to a weeknight replacement for chicken.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4">
        <a href="/table/vegan-eating/" class="btn btn-primary">
          An Introduction to Vegan Eating
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- The thing a website must not get wrong, and an earlier draft
         did: it said the fast "bends". It does not. We bend to the will
         of God; we do not bend His rule to our wants. What can happen
         is that a priest sets a different rule for a particular person
         who cannot keep this one — which is his to do and nobody
         else's, least of all a website's. -->
    <section class="mt-12 border-l-2 border-sacred-gold bg-cream-card p-5">
      <h2 class="font-display text-base text-text-primary">If you cannot keep the fast</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-primary/85">
        The fast does not bend. If you are unwell, pregnant or nursing, caring
        for a child, elderly, or doing heavy work, speak with
        <strong>Fr. Antonios</strong> — not with a website, and not with a
        well-meaning parishioner. He is the only one who can set a different
        rule for you, and he will set it to your circumstances.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="mt-10 border-t border-border-soft pt-6 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      Looking for what today allows? The
      <a href="/calendar/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">parish calendar</a>
      says plainly whether it is a fast day and whether fish, wine or oil are
      permitted. Ready to cook?
      <a href="/table/fasting/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue-dark">Open the fasting book →</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>
